{"term":"porządna kobieta (jako odwrotność)","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"female_virtue_binary","concept":"female_honor_ideal","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The normative category 'decent/respectable woman' invoked as the virtuous pole of the Madonna/whore binary; naming it implicitly shames any woman who deviates from its standard.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"The parenthetical 'jako odwrotność' (as the opposite) signals that research interest is in the phrase's function, not its surface meaning: 'porządna kobieta' operates as a shaming baseline against which sexually active women are measured and found wanting. The phrase is itself unmarked; the shame work is done entirely by contrast."}
{"term":"nieskromna","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"female_immodesty","concept":"wanton_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Feminine adjective meaning immodest or unchaste; applied to women who violate norms of sexual restraint, modesty of dress, or decorous behavior.","english_term":"immodest","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Standard Polish adjective carrying strong moral-religious undertones; in the Catholic Polish context 'skromność' (modesty) is a cardinal female virtue, so its negation is a mild but institutionally loaded accusation. Less crude than direct sexual slurs; its power comes from moral framing rather than vulgarity."}
{"term":"fjut","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vagina","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":85,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":30,"recognition_pct_peak":50,"peak_year":null,"regional":"PL-SL","definition":"Silesian dialect crude term for the vagina, borrowed from South/Central German dialectal Fut or Fud; used both anatomically and as a general insult for a stupid or contemptible person.","english_term":"cunt","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Etymology: South/Central German dialect Fut/Fud (vagina), imported via centuries of German linguistic influence in Upper Silesia. Largely opaque to non-Silesian Polish speakers. The insult use parallels English 'cunt' deployed as a generic contempt term rather than an anatomical reference."}
{"term":"fjuta","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":20,"recognition_pct_peak":40,"peak_year":null,"regional":"PL-SL","definition":"Silesian dialect insult for a promiscuous, slovenly, or disreputable woman; derived or feminized from fjut, functioning analogously to 'slut' or 'tramp' in standard Polish.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Gendered feminine; confined to Upper Silesian dialect and largely unintelligible to non-Silesians. Carries connotations of both sexual looseness and general low character. The -a suffix feminizes the base form fjut."}
{"term":"szlunga","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":15,"recognition_pct_peak":35,"peak_year":null,"regional":"PL-SL","definition":"Silesian dialect slur for a promiscuous, disreputable, or slovenly woman, with connotations of both sexual looseness and personal degradation.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Likely borrowed from German dialect; possible relation to German Schlampe (slob/slut) or a similar etymon. Almost exclusively Silesian; opaque to outside speakers. The -unga suffix is a distinctively regional morphological pattern with no standard Polish equivalent."}
{"term":"szmaciak","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"weak_person_insult","concept":"scumbag","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":50,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":"PL-SL","definition":"A contemptible, spineless, or morally degraded person; masculine noun derived from szmata (rag), implying someone with no self-respect or backbone.","english_term":"scumbag","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"In standard Polish, 'szmata' as an insult skews toward promiscuous women (rag/slut); the -iak suffix masculinizes the concept. In prison and military slang, specifically implies a man who has lost status through submission, betrayal, or failure to resist sexual coercion in the hierarchy."}
{"term":"dupa blada","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"ass_expletive","concept":"shit_expletive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":40,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":"PL-SL","definition":"Silesian exclamation of surprise or annoyance, literally 'pale ass'; used as a mid-level expletive and sometimes as a noun insult for a worthless or ineffectual person.","english_term":"holy crap","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The modifier 'blada' (pale) softens the crude base 'dupa' (ass), creating a characteristically Silesian euphemistic buffer. Has migrated into broader Polish colloquial speech. Can also stand alone as a noun insult meaning an ineffectual, useless fool, distinct from its exclamatory use."}
{"term":"kurołap","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"womanizer","concept":"womanizer","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":45,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":25,"recognition_pct_peak":40,"peak_year":null,"regional":"PL-SL","definition":"Silesian compound noun literally meaning 'hen-catcher'; used pejoratively for a man who obsessively and indiscriminately pursues women.","english_term":"womanizer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Plays on 'kura' (hen; colloquially a woman) + 'łapać' (to catch/grab). The chicken-theft literal meaning gives the word a comic, rustic register alongside its sexual sense. Mostly opaque outside Silesia. Less severe than standard Polish 'kobieciarz'; the rural-animal framing adds ridicule."}
{"term":"cwel","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"prison_sexual_subordinate","concept":"prison_sexual_subordinate","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"slang_crude","crude":98,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Prison slang for a man who has been sexually subordinated through rape or coercion and occupies the lowest, permanently degraded caste in prison hierarchy; in civilian use, an extreme insult implying homosexuality, cowardice, and complete worthlessness.","english_term":"prison bitch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the harshest insults in Polish. Within grypsera, the cwel is ritually untouchable — food, utensils, and clothing cannot be shared with him. The status is permanent and irrevocable once assigned. Fully crossed into civilian speech as a general extreme insult. 'Prison bitch' captures degradation and sexual coercion but not the rigid caste permanence of the Polish concept."}
{"term":"grypsera","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"prison_argot","concept":"prison_subculture_argot","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"subculture","crude":20,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The secret cant or argot of the dominant prisoner class in Polish prisons, encoding a strict caste hierarchy that governs sexual status, rituals of degradation, and social taboos.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"From 'gryps' (a secret note passed in prison, itself from German Grips = wit). Grypsera is not a single term but a complete linguistic register; its sexual vocabulary is the mechanism by which the dominant class ('grypsmen' or 'gitowcy') classifies and controls subordinated prisoners ('cwelowie'). Included here as the source system generating Polish prison sexual slang, not as a sexual term itself."}
{"term":"brać na gwałt","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"rape","concept":"ravishment","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":95,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Phrasal verb meaning to rape someone; literally 'to take by force'; in prison slang specifically refers to coercive sexual assault used to subordinate a victim and assign cwel status.","english_term":"to rape","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Mapped to 'ravishment' as closest registry concept, though ravishment is archaic and sometimes euphemistic; 'brać na gwałt' is unambiguously contemporary and non-consensual. 'Gwałt' alone is the standard Polish legal and clinical term for rape; the phrasal form adds the predatory 'taking' framing dominant in prison usage."}
{"term":"brać z tyłu","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"anal_penetration","concept":"anal_sex","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude phrasal verb meaning to penetrate someone anally from behind; in prison slang specifically refers to the dominant sexual act through which cwel status is assigned to the victim.","english_term":"to take from behind","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'to take from behind.' Context disambiguates: in civilian use it can cover doggy-style vaginal penetration; in prison and military slang the anal and coercive reading dominates. The phrasing emphasizes the active subject's dominance rather than naming the anatomical act."}
{"term":"cipciuch","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"pussy_coward","concept":"pussy_coward","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":50,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Contemptuous augmentative of cipka (vagina); used as a crude insult for a weak, cowardly, or sexually submissive man, directly parallel to English 'pussy' used as a cowardice insult.","english_term":"pussy (coward)","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The -uch suffix marks contemptuous augmentation rather than affection (cf. diminutive cipeczka). In prison slang, applied to men who fail to resist sexual subordination in the hierarchy. The genital-to-cowardice mapping mirrors standard Polish 'ciota' and broad cross-linguistic patterns of feminized anatomy as cowardice insult."}
{"term":"dupa do ściany","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"prison_survival_idiom","concept":"prison_sexual_vulnerability_posture","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"slang_crude","crude":55,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'ass to the wall'; a prison idiom for keeping one's back against the wall to prevent being grabbed and raped from behind; used broadly as an expression of extreme vulnerability requiring constant defensive vigilance.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Encodes the sexual threat environment of Polish prisons in a single spatial image. Has migrated into civilian speech as a general idiom for being cornered with no allies or escape route. No English phrase captures both the specific prison-rape referent and the embodied spatial survival posture simultaneously."}
